<body>
  <Header />
  <main>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">Wireless Headphones</span>
      <h1>Sony WH-1000XM5 vs Sennheiser Momentum 4: Which Headphone Is Better in 2026?</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated July 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      The Sony WH-1000XM5 and the Sennheiser Momentum 4 Wireless are the two premium wireless headphones most often shortlisted in 2026, and they take opposite approaches. Sony wins on noise cancellation, call quality, and smart features. Sennheiser wins on sound quality, battery life, and value. This comparison breaks down ANC, audio, battery, comfort, mic quality, pricing, and use cases so you can pick the right headphone for your workflow.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-top">
      <a href="https://www.sony.com/wh-1000xm5" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View Sony WH-1000XM5</a>
      <a href="https://www.sennheiser-hearing.com/en-US/products/headphones/momentum-4-wireless/" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View Sennheiser Momentum 4</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Sony WH-1000XM5</th><th>Sennheiser Momentum 4</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Best For</td><td>ANC, travel, calls, smart features</td><td>Sound quality, battery life, value</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Price</td><td>$399</td><td>$299</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Driver Size</td><td>30mm carbon fiber composite</td><td>42mm</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Battery Life (ANC on)</td><td>30 hours</td><td>60 hours</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Noise Cancellation</td><td>Class-leading (8 mics, 2 processors)</td><td>Very good (adaptive)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bluetooth</td><td>5.2, multipoint, LDAC</td><td>5.2, multipoint, aptX Adaptive</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Quick Charge</td><td>3 min = 3 hours</td><td>10 min = 6 hours</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Weight</td><td>250g</td><td>293g</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Foldable</td><td>No (flat-folding only)</td><td>Yes (folds into case)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Call Quality</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Good</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Codec Support</td><td>SBC, AAC, LDAC</td><td>SBC, AAC, aptX Adaptive</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Smart Features</td><td>Adaptive Sound, Speak-to-Chat</td><td>Sound Zones, Transparent Hearing</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is the Sony WH-1000XM5?</h2>
    <p>The Sony WH-1000XM5 is a premium wireless headphone built by Sony, the Japanese electronics giant founded in 1946 and based in Tokyo. The WH-1000XM5 launched in May 2022 as the fifth generation of the line that effectively defined the modern noise-canceling headphone category, and by 2026 it remains the benchmark for adaptive noise cancellation, call quality, and smart features.</p>
    <p>The WH-1000XM5's defining feature is its noise cancellation system. Eight microphones and two dedicated processors — one for noise cancellation and one for audio processing — analyze and suppress ambient sound in real time. The headphone also includes adaptive sound control that adjusts settings based on your location and activity, speak-to-chat that pauses music when you talk, and a lightweight 250g design that is comfortable for all-day wear. Pricing is $399, and the headphone supports LDAC for high-res audio streaming on Android devices.</p>

    <h2>What Is the Sennheiser Momentum 4?</h2>
    <p>The Sennheiser Momentum 4 Wireless is a premium wireless headphone built by Sennheiser, the German audio company founded in 1945 and based in Wedemark. The Momentum 4 launched in August 2022 as the fourth generation of Sennheiser's Momentum wireless line, and by 2026 it is the leading choice for audiophiles who prioritize sound quality, battery life, and a refined music-first experience over raw noise cancellation.</p>
    <p>The Momentum 4's defining feature is its sound quality and battery life. Sennheiser's audiophile DNA shows in a tuning that is warmer, more detailed, and more naturally balanced than Sony's bass-forward signature. The 42mm drivers deliver a wider soundstage with better instrument separation. The class-leading 60-hour battery life with ANC enabled is double the Sony's 30 hours. Pricing is $299, $100 less than the Sony, and the headphone supports aptX Adaptive for low-latency, high-quality streaming on Android.</p>

    <h2>Active Noise Cancellation</h2>
    <p>Active noise cancellation is where the Sony WH-1000XM5 establishes a clear lead. Sony's system uses eight microphones and two processors to suppress ambient sound in real time, while Sennheiser's adaptive ANC is good but a step behind.</p>

    <h3>Sony Eight-Microphone System</h3>
    <p>Sony's system is the most effective consumer ANC available in 2026. It handles low-frequency airplane engine drone, mid-range office chatter, and high-frequency HVAC hiss with remarkable consistency. The adaptive sound control automatically adjusts ANC strength based on your location, so it strengthens on a plane and relaxes in a quiet office. For frequent flyers and commuters, Sony's ANC is the category leader.</p>

    <h3>Sennheiser Adaptive ANC</h3>
    <p>The Momentum 4's adaptive ANC is good and does a credible job on commutes and in cafés, but it sits a clear step behind Sony on the hardest noise sources like airplane cabins and busy trains. It handles steady low-frequency hums well but struggles more with variable mid-range noise like conversation. For users who need ANC for casual environments, the Momentum 4 is adequate; for travelers who need silence, Sony wins.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best noise cancellation goes to <span class="winner-tag">Sony WH-1000XM5</span>.</p>

    <h2>Sound Quality</h2>
    <p>Sound quality is where the Sennheiser Momentum 4 pulls ahead. Sennheiser's audiophile tuning is warmer, more detailed, and more naturally balanced than Sony's mainstream, bass-forward signature.</p>

    <h3>Sennheiser 42mm Drivers</h3>
    <p>The Momentum 4's 42mm drivers deliver a wider soundstage with better instrument separation, and vocals sit more forward in the mix in a way that flatters acoustic, jazz, and vocal-centric music. The tuning is warm but not muddy, with a natural balance that rewards critical listening. For audiophiles, the Momentum 4 is the better-sounding headphone out of the box.</p>

    <h3>Sony 30mm Carbon Fiber Drivers</h3>
    <p>The Sony WH-1000XM5 is no slouch — its 30mm carbon fiber drivers are crisp and dynamic — but its default tuning emphasizes bass and treble in a way that is more immediately exciting but less accurate. With EQ adjustment in Sony's app, the gap narrows, but out of the box the Sennheiser is the better-sounding headphone for critical listening. Sony's LDAC support does give it an edge for high-res audio streaming on Android, provided your source supports it.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best sound quality goes to <span class="winner-tag">Sennheiser Momentum 4</span>.</p>

    <h2>Battery Life and Charging</h2>
    <p>Battery life is the Momentum 4's decisive advantage. Its 60 hours of playback with ANC enabled is double the Sony's 30 hours, which is genuinely liberating for travel and daily use.</p>

    <h3>Sennheiser 60-Hour Battery</h3>
    <p>The Momentum 4's 60-hour battery means you can go a full week of heavy daily use between charges versus roughly three days on the Sony. A single charge can cover a long-haul flight and a week of commuting with capacity to spare. For travelers and heavy users, this endurance is a meaningful quality-of-life improvement.</p>

    <h3>Sony 30-Hour Battery</h3>
    <p>The Sony's 30-hour battery is adequate for most use cases but requires more frequent charging. Both support quick charging — Sony gives 3 hours from 3 minutes, while Sennheiser gives 6 hours from 10 minutes — but Sennheiser's overall endurance is the clear advantage. If you hate charging, the Momentum 4 wins.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best battery life goes to <span class="winner-tag">Sennheiser Momentum 4</span>.</p>

    <h2>Call Quality and Smart Features</h2>
    <p>Call quality and smart features are where Sony pulls ahead. Sony's microphone array delivers clearer voice pickup, and its smart features are more polished than Sennheiser's.</p>

    <h3>Sony Call Quality</h3>
    <p>Sony's microphone array produces clearer, more natural voice pickup for calls and video meetings. The WH-1000XM5 is a genuine work-from-home tool, handling calls reliably even in noisy environments. Speak-to-chat automatically pauses music when you start talking, and adaptive sound control adjusts settings based on your activity. For remote workers, Sony is the better tool.</p>

    <h3>Sennheiser Call Quality</h3>
    <p>The Momentum 4's call quality is competent but slightly muddier than Sony's. It handles quiet environments well but struggles more in noisy settings. Sennheiser's smart features, including Sound Zones and Transparent Hearing, are functional but less polished than Sony's adaptive sound control. For users who take frequent calls, Sony is the safer choice.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best call quality and smart features goes to <span class="winner-tag">Sony WH-1000XM5</span>.</p>

    <h2>Comfort and Design</h2>
    <p>Comfort is close, with Sony's lighter weight giving it a slight edge for marathon sessions. Both have plush earcups and well-padded headbands.</p>

    <h3>Sony Lightweight Design</h3>
    <p>The Sony WH-1000XM5 weighs 250g, making it lighter than the Sennheiser's 293g. The lighter weight feels less fatiguing across marathon listening sessions. However, the Sony no longer folds compactly — it only lays flat — which means its carrying case is larger than the Sennheiser's. For travel, the larger case is a minor inconvenience.</p>

    <h3>Sennheiser Foldable Design</h3>
    <p>The Momentum 4 weighs 293g, slightly heavier than the Sony, but it folds compactly into a smaller travel case. The heavier weight is noticeable but not uncomfortable for most users, and the foldable design is a genuine advantage for travel. Both headphones have plush earcups and well-padded headbands that are comfortable for all-day wear.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best lightweight comfort goes to <span class="winner-tag">Sony WH-1000XM5</span>. Best travel design goes to <span class="winner-tag">Sennheiser Momentum 4</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pricing Comparison</h2>
    <p>Pricing is one of the biggest differentiators. The Sony WH-1000XM5 costs $399, while the Sennheiser Momentum 4 costs $299. The right choice depends on your priorities.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Sony WH-1000XM5</th><th>Sennheiser Momentum 4</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Launch Price</td><td>$399</td><td>$349</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Current Street Price</td><td>$399</td><td>$299</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sale Price (major events)</td><td>~$329</td><td>~$249</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ANC Quality</td><td>Class-leading</td><td>Very good</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sound Quality</td><td>Good (bass-forward)</td><td>Excellent (warm, detailed)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Battery Life</td><td>30 hours</td><td>60 hours</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Call Quality</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Good</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Codec Support</td><td>SBC, AAC, LDAC</td><td>SBC, AAC, aptX Adaptive</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Value per Dollar</td><td>Strong for travelers</td><td>Strong for audiophiles</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The $100 price gap is meaningful. For the cost of the Sony, you could buy the Momentum 4 and still have $100 left over, or you could view it as paying a $100 premium for Sony's superior noise cancellation and call quality. Both discount regularly during major shopping events, but the Momentum 4 often drops below $250, making it an exceptional deal, while the Sony rarely falls far below $329.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best value goes to <span class="winner-tag">Sennheiser Momentum 4</span>. Best premium ANC and call quality goes to <span class="winner-tag">Sony WH-1000XM5</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pros and Cons</h2>

    <h3>Sony WH-1000XM5 Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Best-in-class active noise cancellation for travel and offices</li>
        <li>Excellent call quality with crisp, natural voice pickup</li>
        <li>Lightweight 250g design comfortable for long sessions</li>
        <li>Smart features like adaptive sound control and speak-to-chat</li>
        <li>LDAC support for high-res audio on Android</li>
        <li>Quick charging: 3 minutes for 3 hours</li>
        <li>Bluetooth multipoint for two-device switching</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Sony WH-1000XM5 Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Battery life of 30 hours trails the Momentum 4 by half</li>
        <li>No longer folds compactly, larger carrying case</li>
        <li>Sound is tuned for mainstream appeal, less neutral than Sennheiser</li>
        <li>$399 price is $100 more than the Momentum 4</li>
        <li>Smart features can feel intrusive without tuning</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Sennheiser Momentum 4 Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Class-leading 60-hour battery life with ANC on</li>
        <li>Superior sound quality with warm, detailed signature</li>
        <li>$100 cheaper than the Sony WH-1000XM5</li>
        <li>Folds compactly into a smaller travel case</li>
        <li>aptX Adaptive support for low-latency, high-quality streaming</li>
        <li>42mm drivers deliver wider soundstage and better instrument separation</li>
        <li>Bluetooth multipoint for two-device switching</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Sennheiser Momentum 4 Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Noise cancellation is good but a step behind Sony</li>
        <li>Heavier at 293g, slightly less comfortable for very long wear</li>
        <li>Call quality is competent but not as crisp as Sony's</li>
        <li>Smart features less polished than Sony's adaptive sound control</li>
        <li>No LDAC support for Sony ecosystem users</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Should Use Which?</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Frequent Travelers and Commuters</h3>
      <p>If you fly often or commute on noisy transit, the Sony WH-1000XM5 is the better choice. Its class-leading ANC silences airplane engines and train noise more effectively than any competitor, and its lightweight design is comfortable for long flights. The adaptive sound control automatically adjusts ANC strength based on your location, making it the best travel headphone available.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Audiophiles and Music Lovers</h3>
      <p>If you prioritize sound quality above all, the Sennheiser Momentum 4 is the better headphone. Its 42mm drivers and warm, detailed tuning deliver a more natural, audiophile-pleasing sound than Sony's bass-forward signature. For critical listening on acoustic, jazz, and vocal-centric music, the Momentum 4 is the more satisfying headphone.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Remote Workers Who Take Calls</h3>
      <p>If you take frequent calls or video meetings from your headphones, the Sony WH-1000XM5 is the safer choice. Its microphone array produces clearer, more natural voice pickup, and speak-to-chat pauses music automatically when you talk. For remote workers, Sony is a genuine work-from-home tool, not just a music headphone.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Budget-Conscious Buyers</h3>
      <p>If you want a premium headphone without spending $400, the Sennheiser Momentum 4 is the stronger value. At $299, it delivers superior sound quality, class-leading battery life, and competent noise cancellation for $100 less than the Sony. For music-focused listeners who do not need top-tier ANC, the Momentum 4 is the better deal.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Heavy Users Who Hate Charging</h3>
      <p>If you hate charging your headphones, the Sennheiser Momentum 4 is the clear choice. Its 60-hour battery means you can go a full week of heavy daily use between charges, versus roughly three days on the Sony. For travelers who forget chargers or heavy users who want set-and-forget endurance, the Momentum 4 wins decisively.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Silencing a long-haul flight</h3>
      <p>A frequent flyer takes a 12-hour international flight and needs to sleep and work in silence. The Sony WH-1000XM5's eight-microphone ANC system suppresses airplane engine drone more effectively than any competitor, creating a near-silent environment for sleep or focus. The 30-hour battery covers the full flight with capacity to spare. The Momentum 4's ANC is good but lets more engine noise through, making the Sony the clear choice for frequent flyers.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Critical listening at home</h3>
      <p>An audiophile wants a wireless headphone for evening listening sessions on jazz, classical, and acoustic music. The Sennheiser Momentum 4's 42mm drivers deliver a wider soundstage with better instrument separation, and its warm, detailed tuning flatters acoustic and vocal-centric music. The 60-hour battery means weeks of evening listening between charges. The Sony is competent but its bass-forward tuning is less satisfying for critical listening.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Taking calls from a home office</h3>
      <p>A remote worker takes 5 to 10 video calls per day from a home office with background noise from family and pets. The Sony WH-1000XM5's microphone array delivers clearer, more natural voice pickup, and its ANC suppresses background noise on calls. Speak-to-chat pauses music automatically when the worker starts talking. The Momentum 4's call quality is competent but slightly muddier in noisy environments.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Commuting without frequent charging</h3>
      <p>A commuter uses headphones 3 hours per day on trains and buses and hates remembering to charge. The Sennheiser Momentum 4's 60-hour battery covers 20 days of commuting between charges, versus 10 days on the Sony. For users who want set-and-forget endurance, the Momentum 4 is the clear choice. The Sony's superior ANC is a benefit on transit, but the battery advantage tips the balance for forgetful chargers.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Working across a laptop and phone</h3>
      <p>A knowledge worker switches between a laptop for video meetings and a phone for music throughout the day. Both headphones support Bluetooth multipoint, so either can connect to both devices simultaneously and switch automatically. The Sony's superior call quality gives it the edge for the video meetings, while the Sennheiser's superior sound quality gives it the edge for music. For mixed use, Sony is the better all-rounder.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p><strong>Best overall for noise cancellation and calls: Sony WH-1000XM5.</strong> Sony's adaptive ANC is the best consumer noise cancellation available, its microphone array produces clearer calls, and its smart features are more polished. At $399, it is not cheap, but for frequent travelers, commuters, and remote workers who live in their headphones, the premium is well justified.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for sound quality and battery: Sennheiser Momentum 4.</strong> The Momentum 4's 60-hour battery, warmer and more detailed sound, and $299 price make it the value pick of the premium category. For music-focused listeners who do not need class-leading ANC, it is arguably the more satisfying headphone to listen to.</p>
      <p><strong>Best budget choice for audiophiles: Sennheiser Momentum 4.</strong> At $299 (and often $249 on sale), the Momentum 4 delivers superior sound and battery for $100 less than the Sony. For budget-conscious audiophiles, it is the stronger value proposition.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended combo:</strong> For users who want the best of both, use the Sony WH-1000XM5 for travel and calls where ANC and mic quality matter most, and the Sennheiser Momentum 4 for home listening where sound quality and battery endurance matter most. Total cost is $698, which is less than some single flagship headphones.</p>
    </div>

