    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is the Sony WH-1000XM5 better than the Sennheiser Momentum 4?</h3>
    <p>The Sony WH-1000XM5 is better for noise cancellation, call quality, and smart features, making it the top pick for commuters and frequent travelers. The Sennheiser Momentum 4 is better for audiophiles who prioritize sound quality and battery life, offering 60 hours of playback and a warmer, more detailed sound signature for $100 less. Both are excellent flagship headphones that optimize for different priorities.</p>

    <h3>Which has better noise cancellation, the Sony WH-1000XM5 or the Sennheiser Momentum 4?</h3>
    <p>The Sony WH-1000XM5 has the stronger noise cancellation. Its eight microphones and dual processors suppress low-frequency engine rumble and mid-range office noise more effectively than the Momentum 4, which is good but sits a step behind the class-leading Sony and Bose QuietComfort models. For frequent flyers and commuters, Sony is the clear ANC winner.</p>

    <h3>Which has better battery life, the Sony WH-1000XM5 or the Sennheiser Momentum 4?</h3>
    <p>The Sennheiser Momentum 4 wins on battery life with up to 60 hours of playback with ANC on, compared to the Sony WH-1000XM5's 30 hours. If you want to charge once a week instead of every few days, the Momentum 4 is the clear choice. Both support quick charging, but Sennheiser's endurance is genuinely liberating for travel.</p>

    <h3>Which headphone is better for calls and video meetings?</h3>
    <p>The Sony WH-1000XM5 is better for calls and video meetings. Its microphone array delivers clearer, more natural voice pickup than the Sennheiser Momentum 4, which is competent but slightly muddier. For remote workers who take frequent calls from their headphones, Sony's superior call quality is a meaningful advantage.</p>

    <h3>Do both headphones support Bluetooth multipoint?</h3>
    <p>Yes, both the Sony WH-1000XM5 and the Sennheiser Momentum 4 support Bluetooth multipoint, which lets you connect to two devices simultaneously and switch between them automatically. Both also support high-res audio codecs: Sony supports LDAC, while Sennheiser supports aptX Adaptive, so Android users get high-quality streaming on either.</p>

    <h3>Which headphone is better for iPhone users?</h3>
    <p>Both work well with iPhone over standard AAC Bluetooth, but neither supports Apple's proprietary features like spatial audio or automatic device switching with Apple ecosystem. For iPhone users, the choice comes down to priorities: Sony for noise cancellation and calls, Sennheiser for sound quality and battery. Neither has a clear iPhone-specific advantage over AirPods Max.</p>

